+### JOB-report-word-frequencies.R1 · Print each word with its count
+
+Status: built · Intents: none · Source: recon
+
+`wordfreq FILE` prints one line for each distinct word in the file. Each line is the word, one space, and the count. The most common word comes first.
+
+- Given a readable file with the text `the cat and the hat and the bat`, when the user runs `python3 -m wordfreq FILE`, then standard output is exactly `the 3\nand 2\nbat 1\ncat 1\nhat 1\n`, standard error is empty, and the exit code is 0.
+
+### JOB-report-word-frequencies.R2 · Empty file prints nothing
+
+Status: built · Intents: none · Source: recon
+
+A file with no words gives no output and exit code 0.
+
+- Given a readable empty file, when the user runs `wordfreq FILE`, then standard output is empty, standard error is empty, and the exit code is 0.
+
+### JOB-report-word-frequencies.R3 · Unreadable file gives an error
+
+Status: built · Intents: none · Source: recon
+
+If the file cannot be read, the command prints one line to standard error and exits with code 2. Standard output stays empty.
+
+- Given the path `/no/such/file.txt` does not exist, when the user runs `wordfreq /no/such/file.txt`, then standard error is exactly `error: cannot read /no/such/file.txt\n`, standard output is empty, and the exit code is 2.
+
+### JOB-report-word-frequencies.R4 · Wrong number of arguments prints usage
+
+Status: ready · Intents: IN-0001
+
+The command needs exactly one FILE, after the `--top` option and its value are removed. With none, or with more than one, it prints a usage line to standard error and exits with code 2.
+
+- Given no arguments, when the user runs `wordfreq`, then standard error is exactly `usage: wordfreq FILE\n`, standard output is empty, and the exit code is 2.
+- Given two files, when the user runs `wordfreq a.txt b.txt`, then standard error is exactly `usage: wordfreq FILE\n`, standard output is empty, and the exit code is 2.
+- Given only the option, when the user runs `wordfreq --top 3`, then standard error is exactly `usage: wordfreq FILE\n`, standard output is empty, and the exit code is 2.
+
+### JOB-report-word-frequencies.R5 · Run as a module
+
+Status: built · Intents: none · Source: recon
+
+`python3 -m wordfreq FILE` runs the command. The process exit code is the code that the command returns.
+
+- Given the project root is the working directory, when the user runs `python3 -m wordfreq notes.txt` on a missing file, then the exit code of the process is 2.
+
+### JOB-report-word-frequencies.R6 · Print only the top N words
+
+Status: ready · Intents: IN-0001
+
+With `--top N`, the command prints only the N most common words, in the same order and format as without the option. The option can stand before or after FILE (assumed). If the file has fewer than N distinct words, the command prints all of them (assumed). N is valid only when it is made of digits and its value is above zero (assumed), so `03` is 3. For any other N, including a missing N, the command prints `error: --top needs a whole number greater than zero` to standard error, prints nothing to standard output, and exits with code 2. This check comes before the file is read (assumed).
+
+- Given a file with the text `the cat and the hat and the bat`, when the user runs `wordfreq --top 2 FILE`, then standard output is exactly `the 3\nand 2\n`, standard error is empty, and the exit code is 0.
+- Given the same file, when the user runs `wordfreq FILE --top 1`, then standard output is exactly `the 3\n` and the exit code is 0.
+- Given the same file, when the user runs `wordfreq --top 4 FILE`, then standard output is exactly `the 3\nand 2\nbat 1\ncat 1\n`.
+- Given the same file, when the user runs `wordfreq --top 5 FILE`, then standard output is exactly `the 3\nand 2\nbat 1\ncat 1\nhat 1\n`.
+- Given the same file, when the user runs `wordfreq --top 99 FILE`, then standard output is exactly `the 3\nand 2\nbat 1\ncat 1\nhat 1\n` and the exit code is 0.
+- Given the same file, when the user runs `wordfreq --top 03 FILE`, then standard output is exactly `the 3\nand 2\nbat 1\n`.
+- Given an empty file, when the user runs `wordfreq --top 3 FILE`, then standard output is empty and the exit code is 0.
+- Given the same file, when the user runs `wordfreq --top 0 FILE`, then standard error is exactly `error: --top needs a whole number greater than zero\n`, standard output is empty, and the exit code is 2.
+- Given the same file, when the user runs `wordfreq --top N FILE` with N as `-1`, `2.5`, `abc`, `1e2`, `+3`, or an empty string, then standard error is exactly `error: --top needs a whole number greater than zero\n`, standard output is empty, and the exit code is 2.
+- Given the same file, when the user runs `wordfreq FILE --top`, then standard error is exactly `error: --top needs a whole number greater than zero\n`, standard output is empty, and the exit code is 2.
+- Given the path `/no/such/file.txt` does not exist, when the user runs `wordfreq --top 0 /no/such/file.txt`, then standard error is exactly `error: --top needs a whole number greater than zero\n` and the exit code is 2.
+- Given the path `/no/such/file.txt` does not exist, when the user runs `wordfreq --top 2 /no/such/file.txt`, then standard error is exactly `error: cannot read /no/such/file.txt\n` and the exit code is 2.
